# Counterfactual: the adjudicator-independence conjunct stripped from the supplement rule
#
# Derivation is monotone and probe facts load on top, so no probe against the
# real constitution can show what `~($a = $carrier)` prevents. This copy is the
# only way that claim is executed rather than argued: one conjunct removed, and
# the carrier adjudicating the event it would have to pay for starts counting.
:expect-pins 3
# The carrier finds the peril itself. Against the real constitution this derives
# nothing; here it derives the supplement.
pay(Nil, Contribution, Carrier, SchemeA).
authorized(Carrier, InsuranceAdjudicator, Nil).
observe(Carrier, Illness, Nil, SchemeA).
? insure(Carrier, Nil, Illness).
# => TRUE
# The independent route still works, so this fixture removes a guard rather
# than the rule — the difference a stale or over-broad fixture would hide.
pay(Oke, Contribution, Carrier, SchemeA).
authorized(Assessor, InsuranceAdjudicator, Oke).
observe(Assessor, Illness, Oke, SchemeA).
? insure(Carrier, Oke, Illness).
# => TRUE
# Only the supplement rule lost its guard. The guarantee route still refuses a
# carrier-adjudicated event, which is what makes the flip above attributable to
# the one conjunct.
observe(Court, CarrierInsolvency, Carrier, SchemeA).
? insure(PublicGuarantee, Nil, Illness).
# => FALSE
